WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.420
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Good, thank you very much.
May we then turn, please, to item six on the

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:15.300
agenda, which is the corporate and NHS performance
report? Both Karen and Richard will talk to

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.720
this. Karen I think first on the corporate
side, and then I will invite Richard to talk

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:21.900
to the NHS performance.

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:27.460
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes, thank you. I am not
intending to go into detail on all of the

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:34.930
items recorded here. I did want to say that
the focus at this time of the year is obviously

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:45.250
how we are getting on in meeting our delivery
commitments by end of the year. The amber

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:50.530
red reports that you see here reflect the
risks associated with that and delivery challenges.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:57.950
However, be assured that we that are working
really hard to mitigate those, and are on

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.140
course with many of those action plans.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:12.400
It is also worth pointing out that we are
now in the process of developing our business

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:17.470
plan, and these corporate priority programmes
will continue into next year and we are working

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:22.399
up our business plan for each of these programmes,
not only for next year but for the next three

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:31.740
years. This will help us feed into and help
inform the work that we will be doing with

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:41.920
local health economies in terms of developing
their own three year sustainability and transformation

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.640
plans.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:46.820
So that is critically important work, and
I think the stability from this year to next

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.310
year on those corporate priorities will absolutely
help to mitigate some of the risks associated

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:54.939
with this, so I think that is really important.
Just to reflect Cally's point, all of this

00:01:54.939 --> 00:02:00.049
is in a cash constrained environment. So that
is one of our challenges.

00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:05.189
So, again, I was not going to talk about the
risks but I am very happy to take any questions

00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:11.410
that you may have on those as well.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.810
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Before we turn to Richard,
are there questions for Karen on that part

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:21.610
of the report? We can come back to it subsequently
if not now. Margaret?

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:30.110
&gt;&gt;Margaret Casely Hayford: Okay. I find your
reports and the breakdowns very helpful, and

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:35.050
I am loath to add to your burden, but what
I think might be useful would be to have a

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:44.730
metric about A&amp;E broken down so that there
is some sort of focus on mental health presentation

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:46.860
at A&amp;E.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:56.940
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: I will ask Richard, please,
to take that, shall I?

00:02:56.940 --> 00:03:01.920
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Richard, you might want to
come to that question but may we take your

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:02.420
comments first?

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:03.100
&gt;&gt;Richard Barker: Thank you, Chairman. It
is a mixed picture to report, I am afraid.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:07.710
I will try and highlight some of the key indicators
that matter most to patients; some are going

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:11.490
quite well, some perhaps not as well as we
would like. So, if I can start with one of

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:16.290
the ones that is troubling us, and it is the
A&amp;E indicator, an indicator that requires

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:21.580
us to either treat, admit or discharge patients
within four hours of their arrival at the

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:28.320
A&amp;E department. In November last year, we
achieved that for 91.4% of our patients, which

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.930
is somewhat short of the target. That is consistent
with the trend for the last 18 months or so,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:38.840
which is of concern to us all. Our tactics
at the moment are to hold the best possible

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:43.190
position we can for the rest of this winter
but also, working with our colleagues in NHS

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:47.940
Improvement and also with the support of our
new National Director for Urgent Care Pauline

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:53.260
Philip, work on a really urgent strategy to
turn around the position.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.650
That will be done through a range of means.
One concern we do have - and we have heard

00:03:56.650 --> 00:04:01.350
a lot about it already today - is the degree
of variation in performance across England.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:05.230
Many systems and organisations are performing
very well but a number are performing very

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:11.070
poorly, and we need to make sure that we can
address that variation. We are also aware

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:14.910
of our urgent and emergency care strategy,
which has a number of key initiatives which

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:20.479
we have already implemented in some places.
They need to be now urgently brought forward

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:26.789
and expanded and scaled across England. So
a concerted drive on both of those things

00:04:26.789 --> 00:04:32.389
to see significant improvements in the new
financial year, and we can come back to that

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:33.800
in future meetings.

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:39.580
It is worth just building on the Chief Executive's
comments earlier on. Clearly these data are

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:44.340
for November; we have just gone through the
holiday period and the December period, which

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:49.650
is always our most challenging month. We will
not get the results for that period until

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:54.479
next month's Board meeting. I think we can
anticipate some further deterioration in the

00:04:54.479 --> 00:05:00.870
overall four hour wait, because it is always
our most difficult month, but I think we should

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:05.900
reflect on the intelligence that we have about
much better performance in relation to the

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:10.430
fewer serious operational pressures we had
over the Christmas and New Year period. That

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:15.499
certainly seems to have manifested itself
in fewer extreme waits, either for ambulance

00:05:15.499 --> 00:05:19.909
delays or for trolley waits. As I say, we
do not have the full data to confirm that

00:05:19.909 --> 00:05:23.050
yet but certainly that is what the indications
tell us.

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:28.740
I think it is important that we pay tribute
to our staff, both for the planning work they

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:33.460
did before the Christmas/New Year period and
also the execution of those plans. As well

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:36.810
as the front line staff, of course, who deal
with this on a day to day basis, I think it

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:41.289
is important we recognise the work of our
own staff in NHS England. There were some

00:05:41.289 --> 00:05:46.469
difficult days, particularly over the holiday;
the directors will recall that flooding was

00:05:46.469 --> 00:05:51.930
quite prevalent in England and we had some
of our staff, on the Boxing Day bank holiday,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:56.150
effectively put in a full day shift to work
on a multi agency basis to ensure that we

00:05:56.150 --> 00:06:00.289
were able to protect patients and the public
from the impact of those floods. So I think

00:06:00.289 --> 00:06:04.120
that is important to recognise.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.490
The next target I want to reflect on is the
18 week target, referral to treatment. In

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:19.469
November we maintained our position of having
no less than 92% of our patients on our waiting

00:06:19.469 --> 00:06:24.270
lists having waited less than 18 weeks. We
maintained that position in November. There

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:28.969
continued to be a lot of pressure on that
target too, a lot of that down to diagnostic

00:06:28.969 --> 00:06:32.210
pressures, which I will come back to in a
moment.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:36.229
The next commitment I would like to refer
to is our 62 day cancer target. We have nine

00:06:36.229 --> 00:06:41.020
cancer targets, and we are actually achieving
eight of them; the one we are struggling with

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:47.379
most is the 62 day target, which is the 62
days from initial referral to first definitive

00:06:47.379 --> 00:06:51.560
treatment. Obviously, we have heard from Cally
about the commitments we have to improve cancer

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:56.419
services in every respect, and that will have
an impact on this target too.

00:06:56.419 --> 00:07:05.139
The figures for November were 83.5% of patients
seen within that 62 day timeframe; 85% is

00:07:05.139 --> 00:07:09.559
our target. The good news is that the November
figures were quite a significant improvement

00:07:09.559 --> 00:07:14.340
on October and I would like to think that,
although I think it is a bit early yet, that

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:21.180
is linked to remedial action plans that were
initiated across all of the poorest performing

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:26.800
systems in England during October and November.
Perhaps the high degree of focus on Cally's

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:30.979
arrival at least drew attention and focus
to this indicator, but we are hoping those

00:07:30.979 --> 00:07:34.610
action plans will actually deliver some more
sustainable performance improvements over

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:39.569
the next few months, notwithstanding the fact
that the initiatives Cally described are needed

00:07:39.569 --> 00:07:43.809
to improve performance overall on a long term
sustainable basis, so it is still a matter

00:07:43.809 --> 00:07:45.629
of tactical and strategic focus.

00:07:45.629 --> 00:07:51.719
I said I would come back to diagnostics, and
again Cally's report gave a lot of understanding

00:07:51.719 --> 00:07:57.309
about the pressures we have in diagnostics.
Our target is that no more than 1% of people

00:07:57.309 --> 00:08:00.909
will wait six weeks for a diagnostic test.
So, although we set ourselves quite a high

00:08:00.909 --> 00:08:04.650
bar on that, nevertheless it is a concern
to any patient waiting over six weeks who

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:10.569
would obviously be anxious about such delays.
The position in November was that 1.6% of

00:08:10.569 --> 00:08:17.139
patients waited over six weeks, so that is
some way outside of our target commitment.

00:08:17.139 --> 00:08:22.009
The major contribution to that has been a
6.8% rise in demand for diagnostics. Some

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:28.569
of that is to be expected through age and
demographic change, but a lot of that increase

00:08:28.569 --> 00:08:33.779
has been, for very good reasons, earlier interventions
and diagnosis, people attending screening

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:39.930
programmes and public awareness: the Be Clear
On Cancer campaign, for example, encouraging

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:43.660
patients to look for signs and symptoms and
present to GPs with those.

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:48.320
So that is good news, but a 6.8% increase
is a significant strain on resources; for

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:54.460
a typical working week, that is two to three
hours' extra work to be added on. So we are

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:58.970
doing something about that: as we heard again
during the cancer presentation, Ian Dodge

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:02.770
is working with Cally on the cancer diagnosis
but also on all the other diagnoses, looking

00:09:02.770 --> 00:09:08.230
at new fresh approaches to both increase capacity
- and we are investing in capacity - but crucially

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:09.160
to increase productivity.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:13.880
They are also looking to smooth flows and
improve choice, so the patients do not get

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:18.160
stuck in long queues in one place when there
is actually capacity in some other part of

00:09:18.160 --> 00:09:22.960
the system to use. We have had some success
in endoscopy in terms of supporting patients

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.440
to move to different providers to be seen
more promptly, but that needs to be extended

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:29.770
much more widely.

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:34.600
Two bits of better news, and it is in relation
to mental health. We set ourselves, around

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:39.470
18 months ago, targets to improve access to
psychological therapies and improve our dementia

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:46.470
diagnosis rates. Both of the targets we set
ourselves - 15% access rate for psychological

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:52.920
therapies and a 67% diagnosis rate for dementia
- have both now been achieved across England,

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:54.500
so we are very, very pleased about that.

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:58.700
There is still more to do in psychological
therapies; we are now focusing on the length

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:04.550
of time it takes to receive a psychological
therapy treatment, and then next year we will

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.860
start looking at that target and focusing
on it specifically, so better news in the

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:08.830
mental health field.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:16.210
Perhaps just to Margaret's question on mental
health presentation in A&amp;E, we have some data

00:10:16.210 --> 00:10:19.700
on that but we do not collect it in a way
that we can nationally describe, of the four

00:10:19.700 --> 00:10:25.720
hour waits, which were waiting for a mental
health condition and what the diagnosis and

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:26.970
outcome of that was.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:30.980
Unfortunately, as I know Margaret is aware
and has drawn attention to before, many people

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:35.960
who attend A&amp;E have multiple problems, or
present with one problem but behind that there

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:40.110
is a mental health issue. It is certainly
an issue in terms of the improvement work

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:46.260
that Pauline will lead for us: making sure
we have really good liaison psychiatry and

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:51.220
rapid access to mental health support, close
to A&amp;E, will allow us to actually give much

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:53.790
better services for people with mental health
problems.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:59.370
Also, for people who are running A&amp;E services,
it gives them access to resource that they

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:04.050
often do not have and it is a real strain
on the operational running of an A&amp;E department.

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:07.490
So that is the end of my report, Chairman,
I am happy to take any questions.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.930
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much. Let
me invite questions and comments from members

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:12.090
of the Board.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:20.800
John, please.

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:29.460
&gt;&gt;John Burn: I was quite interested about
the question of sepsis. Is this a good moment

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:36.950
to raise that? It has been very much in the
headlines this week, and I had a sense of

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:43.980
deja vu. 40 years ago my partner missed a
case, in paediatrics, of a child presenting

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:51.030
with sepsis, and again, in fact, when Richard
and I sat again at the SHA in the North East

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:58.190
we were struggling with working out how to
raise the awareness of it. So perhaps I would

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:07.740
like to ask Bruce: the challenge seems to
be how we pick this out of the crowd, because

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:17.670
it has the capacity to imitate so many normal
situations where people are not critically

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:18.890
ill.

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:25.130
The underlying question is that we are hearing
a great deal about antibiotic resistance,

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:30.250
and whether in fact this is going to become
an ever bigger problem as we get more partially

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:34.370
treated cases as antibiotic resistance becomes
more of a threat to us.

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:44.080
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Thanks very much, John. I mean
you raise this, I suppose, as a consequence

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:51.040
of some of the things that have been in the
media recently, with the death of a young

00:12:51.040 --> 00:12:56.890
child, William. I think I would just like
to preface any remarks by saying that I know

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:01.140
people around this Board, and others who I
have spoken to, are filled with a deep sense

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:06.160
of sadness and sympathy for his family, and
I think that has to be our starting position

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.930
and to try and do something about that.

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:16.260
Over the years I think the clinical professions
have been dogged by a number of so called

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:23.720
'single conditions' which are quite elusive.
They are very difficult; they can be, at one

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:29.060
level, barn door diagnoses and at other levels
they can masquerade as something that is really

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:31.740
quite trivial.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:36.950
We learnt a lot about how to deal with that
when we decided to tackle venous thromboembolism,

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:43.960
which was probably the first big clinical
priority that the NHS addressed in isolation

00:13:43.960 --> 00:13:48.510
in this sort of area, and we made big progress
on that and I think we learnt a lot about

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:55.720
how to do it. As a consequence of the success
of that, senior clinicians around the country

00:13:55.720 --> 00:14:05.380
felt that sepsis should be the next area that
we should tackle, because we all, in clinical

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:12.090
practice, have been haunted by episodes of
sepsis which we have missed and which have

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:22.060
caused devastation to individuals or to their
families, and because it can be quite difficult

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:23.800
to deal with.

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:27.320
One of the things that we learnt from venous
thromboembolism is that there are a lot of

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:33.930
things that we can do in the system - the
way we run our organisations, the way we run

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:39.880
our organisations, the way we run our therapeutic
modalities - that we can change to make it

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:46.480
easier for clinicians to do their job more
easily, and make it more difficult for them

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:54.840
to miss things. So, over the last year or
18 months or so, senior clinicians in NHS

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.920
England have been working with a number of
organisations: the Academy of Medical Royal

00:14:58.920 --> 00:15:04.210
Colleges, the Royal College of Nursing, NICE,
Health Education England, our regional and

00:15:04.210 --> 00:15:09.250
area team medical directors, the Sepsis Trust,
who have done a lot of work on this, to name

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:12.120
but a few; I will have left several names
out.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:19.160
The consequence of that was that we have published
a plan to mobilise, if you like, the whole

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:28.290
of the clinical community and the NHS in general
to help us tackle sepsis. We published that

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:35.560
probably not on the most obvious day of the
year, it was 23rd December last year, but

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:43.120
for those people who need to know about it,
they do. The document focuses on how we can

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:49.160
improve prevention of sepsis, both in primary
care and secondary care, how we can identify

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:55.020
it more quickly and how we can treat it more
quickly. One of the things about sepsis is

00:15:55.020 --> 00:16:01.700
that it can be indolent for a period of time
and it can suddenly burst forth very, very

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:07.230
quickly; so timeliness in all of these things
is really important.

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:16.310
So we are embarking now on a consensus approach
to how we can tackle this condition which

00:16:16.310 --> 00:16:21.530
actually, you have alluded to, is really pretty
tricky. However, I think you are absolutely

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:31.640
right to raise the issue of antibiotic usage.
Around the world there is over use of antibiotics,

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:38.320
not just in a clinical setting but in agricultural
settings and other settings, which are promoting

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:46.880
antibiotic resistance. I think we owe a debt
to Sally Davies, the Chief Medical Officer,

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:53.800
who has brought this to the attention of the
wider community, and we would be very wise

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:56.040
to heed some of our warnings.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:17:00.670
So, one of the things that clinicians are
having to grapple with at the moment is, at

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:06.770
one level, they are trying to be cautious,
so that they are not in the world of over

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:10.720
use of antibiotics. However, equally they
have to get the balance right and not be in

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:17.699
the under use world. So there is a continuing
debate going on and we are working very closely

00:17:17.699 --> 00:17:24.389
with Sally Davies and her team, through the
Chief Pharmaceutical Officer, Keith Ridge

00:17:24.389 --> 00:17:29.259
and the Royal Colleges of Medicine and other
prescribing groups.

00:17:29.259 --> 00:17:39.110
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Okay. Thank you John and
Bruce. Any further questions for Richard or

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:42.919
for Karen? If not, then thank you very much,
both of you.

